[task-01] Before an injected edit's result is allowed to count as evidence, the review
    checks that the edit really makes its named defect true. That check asks two
    questions, in sequence.
    -> evidence-check-defect-truth  [functional/explicit]
       The review checks that an injected edit's result really makes its named defect
       true before counting it as evidence.
    -> two-questions-sequence  [functional/explicit]
       The check asks two questions in sequence.

[task-02] The first asks whether the edit changes the code's behaviour at all. Both
    versions of the code are compared with comments and whitespace removed, so an edit
    that only reformats or re-comments the code is not mistaken for a change. An edit
    that changes no behaviour is refused at this step, without the defect being
    considered.
    -> behavior-change-check  [functional/explicit]
       Determine whether the edit changes the code's behaviour at all.
    -> ignore-comments-and-whitespace-in-comparison  [invariant/explicit]
       Compare both versions of the code with comments and whitespace removed.
    -> reformatting-not-counted-as-change  [invariant/explicit]
       An edit that only reformats or re-comments the code is not mistaken for a change.
    -> no-behavior-change-refusal  [invariant/explicit]
       The step refuses edits that change no behaviour, without considering the defect.

[task-03] Only an edit that does change behaviour reaches the second question, which
    asks whether the code had the defect's expected behaviour before the edit and has
    its defective behaviour after it. An edit that fails either half is refused,
    including one that repairs a defect the code already had.
    -> change-behaviour-to-reach-second-question  [functional/explicit]
       An edit must change behaviour to reach the second question.
    -> regression-defect-behavior-before-after  [regression/explicit]
       The code had the defect's expected behaviour before the edit and has its
       defective behaviour after it.
    -> edit-must-fail-either-half-to-be-refused  [invariant/explicit]
       An edit is refused when it fails either half of the second-question check.
    -> repairs-existing-defect  [regression/explicit]
       The change includes an edit that repairs a defect the code already had.

[task-04] An edit is verified only when it passes both. When an edit is refused, the
    review records which of the two questions refused it and why, so that the two can be
    told apart wherever refusals are counted.
    -> passes-both-questions  [invariant/explicit]
       An edit is verified only when it passes both questions.
    -> refusal-records-question-and-reason  [functional/explicit]
       When an edit is refused, the review records which of the two questions refused it
       and why.

[task-05] Verification is on by default.
    -> verification-on-by-default  [invariant/explicit]
       Verification is on by default.

